  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on the most probable outcome (i.e., the outcome with the highest probability), which in both lotteries is 0 Euros. Lottery A has a 67% chance of 0 Euros, Lottery B has 66%, so the reason would favor Lottery A due to the higher probability of its most probable outcome. However, the individual instead focused on the probability of the favorable (non-zero) outcome and chose Lottery B because it has a higher chance (34% > 33%) of winning something. This is conceptually different from the decision reason, which emphasizes the most likely outcome (loss) rather than the chance of gain. The individual considered probabilities, but not the same ones emphasized by the decision reason. They did not mention or act on the 67% vs 66% comparison of the most probable (zero) outcomes. Instead, they referenced the higher chance of winning, which aligns more with a 'maximize chance of winning' heuristic than the 'most probable outcome' rule. Therefore, the individual did not use the specified decision reason.",
